\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Process overview of RAP generation before rover departures}}{8}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces SayCan combining language-model relevance scores with value-function affordance scores to select the next skill, reproduced from Ahn et al.~\cite {ahn2022saycan}.}}{9}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces The KnowNo pipeline: an LLM planner proposes next steps with confidence scores, conformal prediction narrows these to a calibrated prediction set, and the rover asks for help whenever that set contains more than one option. Reproduced from \citet {ren2023knowno}.}}{11}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces The introspective planning pipeline: a knowledge base of explained past examples is built offline, and at deployment the most similar example is retrieved to inform the LLM's confidence for each candidate step. Reproduced from \citet {liang2024introspective}.}}{13}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The compute split. The three models and the dialogue run on the brain. The Pi runs only what must keep working without them.}}{18}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces One command from speech to report. Shaded blocks call a model. The dashed block drives the rover. Dashed arrows show the scene digest feeding the two checks that need to know what is in the room.}}{20}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces The dialogue phases up to the start of a mission. A rejection returns to \texttt {CLARIFYING} with the person's reason added to the history.}}{24}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces The recovery loop. The turn counter is shared across the whole mission, so it cannot be used to drag a failing mission on.}}{25}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces How a condition is resolved. An answer the camera cannot support does not pick a branch. It stops the step and goes to the recovery loop.}}{27}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces The free-roam approach. Each cycle is one hop and one look. Every exit that is not \texttt {ok} returns \texttt {blocked} with a reason, and that is what starts recovery.}}{28}{figure.caption.25}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces What the brain actually reaches on the Pi. Everything above \texttt {rosbridge\_websocket} and \texttt {web\_video\_server} is Hiwonder's stock ROS2 stack, started as one unit by \texttt {bringup.launch.py} inside the Pi's Docker container.}}{34}{figure.caption.27}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces \texttt {transcribe\_audio}. The hallucination check exists because \texttt {fluister-turbo} occasionally transcribes silence or background noise as one of a handful of fixed captioning artefacts, most often ``thank you so much for watching''.}}{36}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Synthesis runs on a worker thread because \texttt {\_speak} shares an event loop with \texttt {ingest\_frame}: synthesis is sub-second but still blocking, and perception should not stall mid-mission for a voice line. A failure degrades the mission to text-only rather than stopping it, the same soft-fail stance the pre-departure dialogue's own \texttt {\_speak} in \texttt {pipeline.py} takes.}}{36}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Both roles call the same interface. \texttt {get\_provider(role)} resolves \texttt {PLANNER\_PROVIDER} or \texttt {VLM\_PROVIDER} to a concrete class and caches the instance.}}{37}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces \texttt {generate\_plan}. A smaller model sometimes writes a step as a bare string instead of an object, or nests the list oddly, so every step is checked before anything downstream calls \texttt {.get} on it. \texttt {\_promote\_moves\_to\_approach} rewrites a \texttt {move} step whose target names an object into an \texttt {approach} step (Section~\ref {sec:vocabulary}); the retried transient failures from \texttt {provider.complete()} are omitted here for space and described in the text above.}}{38}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces \texttt {\_ask}. Salvage is only enabled for \texttt {inventory\_frame} and \texttt {survey\_frame}, the two calls that ask for a list of objects and sometimes hit \texttt {num\_predict} mid-list on a model that has started repeating itself; a reply with ten correct objects and one truncated one is more useful than discarding it. Every other call keeps \texttt {salvage=False}, since a truncated yes/no answer or bounding box is not something a partial parse can safely recover.}}{39}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces \texttt {\_advance\_to\_confirmation}, one turn of the clarification loop. The repeat-question check exists because a model that cannot think of anything new to ask would otherwise repeat the same question until the five-turn cap was reached, rather than giving up as soon as it had nothing left to add.}}{40}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces \texttt {run\_mission}'s step loop. The conditional-tag check is what Section~\ref {sec:conditional} relies on: the planner only decides which steps carry a \texttt {when} tag, and this check is what honours it. The loop's own \texttt {finally} block (not shown) writes the report for every non-aborted outcome; an operator-triggered \texttt {asyncio.CancelledError} skips straight to \texttt {rover.halt()} instead.}}{41}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces \texttt {\_guidance\_loop}. A ``no'' out of the turn-count check on the right loops back to ask again rather than ending the mission; running out of turns is itself a third halt reason (\texttt {guidance\_exhausted}), alongside timeout and decline, so a batch of failed runs can be told apart by why they failed.}}{42}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces \texttt {ingest\_frame}'s three tiers, feeding \texttt {revision.classify}. Tier 0 is a 32$\times $32 greyscale thumbnail diff plus a Laplacian-variance blur score (\texttt {PERCEPTION\_MIN\_SHARPNESS}, \texttt {PERCEPTION\_MIN\_INTERVAL\_S}, \texttt {PERCEPTION\_CHANGE\_THRESHOLD}); tier 1 is one \texttt {describe\_frame} caption; tier 2 only fires while executing.}}{43}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces \texttt {\_follow\_line}, the supervisor added around the stock line-follower node. It owns exactly the three things the node has no opinion about: arrival, obstacle stopping through the polled sonar (using \texttt {\_sonar\_seq} to tell two fresh readings from one stale one read twice), and a wall-clock give-up, since the node itself drives forward indefinitely if the line is lost. The \texttt {\_lf\_unavailable} check (added 11 October) means a drive that never started is reported as blocked rather than as a drive that ran and found nothing.}}{44}{figure.caption.37}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces \texttt {VirtualRover.execute\_step}'s dispatch. An \texttt {approach} step can only fail by planning (no path, or an unresolved target logged as a hallucination for RQ2), never by collision; a \texttt {move} or \texttt {turn} step is swept against the same grid and stops at the last free point if blocked, so a collision there means the chain of plan steps itself went somewhere it should not have.}}{45}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.12}{\ignorespaces \texttt {/ws/dialogue} (top pair) carries everything up to confirmation; \texttt {/ws/execution} (bottom pair) carries the mission. One frame per complete utterance, not a stream.}}{46}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.13}{\ignorespaces \texttt {report.py}'s outcome classification. A mission that needed at least one guidance turn is never reported as a plain \texttt {completed}, even if it finished, because the distinction between a clean run and a recovered one (Section~\ref {sec:recovery}) is exactly what RQ1 needs to see; \texttt {recovered} only counts a run that actually reached its target afterwards, not merely one that ended without halting.}}{47}{figure.caption.40}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces (a) Plans with the correct visit order out of three runs, against chain depth, at three context settings (points are offset horizontally for legibility). (b) Projected tokens per planning call against depth, measured to depth 30, with the 1024 and 2048 limits marked.}}{54}{figure.caption.62}%
